  <p class="eyebrow">Explanation</p>
  <h1>Understanding parametric sources</h1>
    <p>
        The traditional approach to designing fonts is to use design masters at each end or in 
        the middle of a spectrum. For example a Hairline, Regular, and Black masters. 
        The result is points being moved in multiple directions at once. In parametric 
        design the goal is to isolate the transformations and control them independently, 
        while treating Regular as the origin of the design space. Such as changing only 
        the weight of the vertical strokes.
    </p>
</header>

<div class="intro-content">
  <section>
    <h2>What this section explains</h2>

    <p>
      These articles provide a framework for reading the archive. They explain
      how parametric axes, and parametric sources
      relate to one another; why a transformation may need different treatment
      in different glyph constructions; and how the resulting decisions can be
      documented and compared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Start with the framework</h2>

    <p>
      <a href="/explanation/parent-axes/">Parent and child axes</a> article
      introduces the distinction between a shared typographic transformation
      and the project-specific controls used to realise it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/explanation/parametric-sources/">Parametric sources</a>
      explains how a source records the design work behind one of those
      controls.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><a href="/reference/glossary">Terminology</a></h2>

    <p>
      A glossary of parametric-source terminology defines the concepts used 
      throughout the archive while assuming familiarity with general type-design
      and interpolation terminology.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From a system to its evidence</h2>

    <p>
      A parametric system is not only a set of axes. It also includes the
      constructions that reveal their behaviour, the adjustments required when
      a rule does not hold cleanly, and the specimens used to assess the
      result.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Reference section records this material for individual projects. It
      begins with the parent and child axes of Amstelvar Avar2, and will
      expand as further projects are studied.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/reference/">Browse the reference</a>
    </p>
  </section>
